          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the mattress profile matters</h3>

          <p>
            Swapping a 10-inch mattress for a 14-inch one raises the bed four inches. The Leesa and Helix are the tall options here; the Purple Restore and TEMPUR-Cloud suit frames that already sit high.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not overshoot. A bed that is too tall forces you to hop down, which is a fall risk. When buying for a parent, measure their bed and their best chair first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="movement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Responsive or Sinking Foam: Which Is Easier to Move On?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ease of movement comes down to how quickly the top layer pushes back. A slow foam holds your shape for a few seconds after you move, so you are always climbing out of a dent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Memory foam</h3>

          <p>
            Memory foam softens with body heat and recovers slowly. That gives deep pressure relief, and it is why owners with hip or knee problems commonly report feeling stuck. A thin memory foam layer over coils is much less of a problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Responsive foam, latex and gel grids</h3>

          <p>
            Responsive polyfoam, latex and Purple's GelFlex Grid spring back quickly. The Helix, Leesa and Brooklyn list responsive foams in their stacks, and owners commonly describe the Purple grid as easy to shift on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Coils add bounce</h3>

          <p>
            Pocketed coils add a push that helps you change position and stand from the edge. If moving in bed is your biggest problem, choose a hybrid and avoid anything described as deep-hugging.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caveat: a very responsive bed can feel less cushioning on bony points. The fix is usually a medium rather than a firm, not a slower foam.
          </p>
        </div>

