        <div id="adjustable-base" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Does the Mattress Work With an Adjustable Base?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Raising the head helps people with reflux or snoring, or who rest upright, and some use it as a halfway point before standing. Raising the knees eases the lower back for some sleepers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What makes a mattress compatible</h3>

          <p>
            The mattress has to bend without damage. Foam and pocketed-coil hybrids generally do; connected innersprings often do not. The picks here are 10 to 14 inches, a range most bases handle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the makers say</h3>

          <p>
            Helix lists its adjustable bases as compatible. Purple says the Restore needs a base tested to at least 750 pounds. Nectar sells the Classic Hybrid bundled with its base. Leesa does not state compatibility, so confirm first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the warranty language</h3>

          <p>
            Some brands require specific support to keep the warranty valid. Brooklyn Bedding asks for solid support or slats no more than 5 inches apart. Read the support rules before putting a new mattress on an old frame or base, and keep both receipts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Will It Sleep Hot, and Can You Move It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many people sleep warmer with age, from hot flashes or medications. Weight matters for a different reason: someone has to unbox the mattress, rotate it and change the sheets, often on a tall bed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sleeping cool</h3>

          <p>
            Coils let air move, so hybrids generally run cooler than all-foam beds. Purple says its grid draws heat away, and Helix offers a GlacioTex cooling cover. Dense memory foam is the most likely to feel warm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight and handling</h3>

          <p>
            Helix lists the queen Midnight Luxe at 115 pounds, Purple the queen Restore at 114, and Leesa the queen Sapira Chill at 103. That is a two-person job, so pay for in-home setup if offered.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flipping and rotating</h3>

          <p>
            None of these is designed to be flipped, because the comfort layers sit on one side. Rotate head to foot every few months, and use deep-pocket sheets that are easy to change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="trials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do Trials and Returns Work, Especially on Amazon?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Direct trials here range from 90 nights at Tempur-Pedic to 365 nights at Helix, Leesa, Brooklyn and Nectar. Read the fine print for minimum nights and fees.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Minimums and fees</h3>

          <p>
            Purple asks for at least 21 nights before a return and deducts transportation charges. Tempur-Pedic charges a $175 return fee. The body takes several weeks to adjust to a new surface, especially with joint pain, so a long trial is worth more than a small discount.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Amazon purchases</h3>

          <p>
            A brand's trial may cover only direct orders. Tempur-Pedic says its trial excludes other retailers, and Leesa's Amazon listings mention 100 or 120 nights versus 365 direct. Check the seller and return terms first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warranties</h3>

          <p>
            Warranties cover defects like deep impressions, not comfort. Helix, Leesa and Brooklyn list limited lifetime, Nectar forever, and Purple and Tempur-Pedic 10 years. Keep the law tag on, register the mattress, and photograph any sag with a straightedge in case you need to file a claim.
          </p>
        </div>

