        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What type of mattress is best for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most seniors, a medium to medium-firm hybrid is the best type. The coils give a firm edge for sitting and standing and a bit of bounce for turning over, while the foam layers cushion shoulders and hips. All-foam memory foam beds offer deep pressure relief but are harder to move on and usually have soft edges.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress height for elderly people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for a total sitting height where your feet rest flat on the floor and your knees sit at about a right angle when you sit on the edge. Measure a chair you stand from easily and match it. Mattress thickness is one lever; the frame and foundation are the others, and they are often easier to change.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a firm or soft mattress better for arthritis?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither extreme. A very firm mattress presses hard on arthritic hips and shoulders, while a very soft one lets the hips sink and makes moving harder. Most people with arthritis do best on medium to medium-firm. Side sleepers usually want a touch softer, back sleepers a touch firmer. Persistent pain warrants a doctor or physical therapist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is memory foam bad for seniors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not bad, but often not ideal. Memory foam relieves pressure well, which helps sore joints, but thick slow-response foam holds you in place and can make turning over and getting up harder. A thin memory foam layer on top of coils, as in many hybrids, keeps most of the cushioning without the stuck feeling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put any mattress on an adjustable bed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Foam and pocketed-coil hybrids usually flex well, but older connected innerspring mattresses often do not, and very thick mattresses can resist the hinge. Check that the maker lists adjustable base compatibility and any weight rating for the base. Purple, for instance, says the Restore needs a base tested to at least 750 pounds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should seniors replace a mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A commonly cited lifespan is seven to ten years, but replace yours sooner if it has visible sagging, if you wake stiffer than you went to bed, or if getting out of bed has become harder. Changes in your body matter too: a bed that suited you at 55 may be too firm or too low at 75.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size mattress is best for an elderly couple?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A queen is the minimum for two adults, and a king gives each sleeper more room to move without disturbing the other. Couples with different firmness needs can use a split king, two twin XL mattresses side by side, which also lets each person use their own side of a split adjustable base.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
